      "title": "Dissecting Advantage-Guided Post-Training for Vision-Language-Action Policies",
      "takeaway": "This work studies how the coupled design choices in advantage-guided post-training of vision-language-action (VLA) policies—how critic-derived advantages are constructed, calibrated, and weighted—affect final policy performance, since existing recipes bundle them into one end-to-end procedure. The authors run a controlled empirical study that separates these choices and introduce stage-specific offline evaluation methods to screen alternatives without exhaustive real-robot evaluation, identifying a modular recipe combining temporal-difference advantage construction, group-wise calibration, and continuous advantage weighting. Across four real-world bimanual tasks, this recipe improves mean task progress and success over the SFT initialization by 0.42 and 0.63, respectively, and the proposed diagnostics show overall alignment with downstream real-world performance. When reading, inspect how the estimands are separated and how the staged offline diagnostics are validated against real-robot outcomes.",
